@meta
id: 29-11
title: One verb, three comment syntaxes
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: commentary, packadd
par: 86
minutes: 8
vscode: it mostly works, and when it does not you have no idea where to look
open: schema.sql README.md tsconfig.json
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall! | set hidden

@brief
The commentary plugin is shipped with this lesson again, in
`pack/dojo/start/commentary/`. Fifty lines, and not one of them knows
what SQL is.

It does not have a table of languages. It reads one buffer-local option,
`commentstring`, which the filetype plugin set when the file was opened.
For TypeScript that option holds `// %s`, for SQL `-- %s`, for markdown
`<!-- %s -->`. The plugin puts your line where the `%s` is. That is the
entire mechanism, and it is why `gcc` does the right thing in a language
the plugin has never heard of.

Three buffers, one line to comment out in each:

  1. `schema.sql` — the `DROP TABLE` at the bottom already ran in staging
     and must not run again.
  2. `README.md` — the sentence about the legacy `/v1/refunds` endpoint is
     no longer true, and we want it out of the rendered page without
     losing it.
  3. `tsconfig.json` — `strictNullChecks` has to come off for one release.

The third one will go wrong, and that is deliberate. Vim ships no
`commentstring` for JSON, because JSON has no comments — but tsconfig is
not really JSON and `//` works there. Set the option for that buffer
before you toggle. Look at what `gcc` gives you if you do not.

@keys
| `:set commentstring?` | show what this buffer will use |
| `:setlocal commentstring=//\ %s` | set it for this buffer only |
| `gcc` | toggle the line |
| `:bn` | next buffer |
| `:wqa` | write and quit all three |

@hints
Two of the three need nothing but `gcc` and a way of getting to the right
line.
--
For the JSON file, `:setlocal commentstring=//\ %s`. The backslash before
the space is how you put a space in a `:set` value.
--
`G` `gcc` `:bn`, `/legacy` `Enter` `gcc` `:bn`,
`:setlocal commentstring=//\ %s` `Enter`, `/strictNull` `Enter` `gcc`,
`:wqa`.

@solution
    G  gcc  :bn<Enter>
    /legacy<Enter>  gcc  :bn<Enter>
    :setlocal commentstring=//\ %s<Enter>
    /strictNull<Enter>  gcc
    :wqa<Enter>

Same three keystrokes in each buffer, three different results:

    -- DROP TABLE refunds_old;
    <!-- The legacy /v1/refunds endpoint ... -->
    // "strictNullChecks": true,

Note the markdown one wrapped the line on both sides. The plugin split
`commentstring` at the `%s` and used whatever was on each side, so a
two-sided comment syntax costs it no extra code.

`setlocal` matters here and `set` would have been wrong: `commentstring`
is buffer-local, so a global `set` would leak the JSON setting into
whatever you opened next. When a fix is about *this file*, `setlocal` is
almost always the one you want. And if you find yourself typing it in
every tsconfig, that is an autocommand for your vimrc:

    autocmd FileType json setlocal commentstring=//\ %s

Which is chapter 27's machinery doing chapter 29's job. Most of what
people call "configuring a plugin" is this: setting the vim options the
plugin already reads, rather than options the plugin invented.

@start pack/dojo/start/commentary/plugin/commentary.vim
" dojo-commentary.vim - a small, honest commenting plugin.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.
"
"   gcc          toggle the comment on this line
"   gc{motion}   toggle the comment on every line the motion covers
"   {Visual}gc   toggle the comment on the selection
"
" It never guesses the comment syntax. It reads 'commentstring', which the
" filetype plugin already set: // %s for typescript, -- %s for sql,
" <!-- %s --> for markdown, # %s for yaml.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_commentary')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_commentary = 1

function! s:parts() abort
  let cms = &commentstring =~# '%s' ? &commentstring : '# %s'
  let at = stridx(cms, '%s')
  return [substitute(strpart(cms, 0, at), '\s*$', '', ''),
        \ substitute(strpart(cms, at + 2), '^\s*', '', '')]
endfunction

function! s:rx(text) abort
  return escape(a:text, '\.*$^~[]/')
endfunction

function! s:toggle(first, last) abort
  let [open, close] = s:parts()
  let head = '^\(\s*\)' . s:rx(open) . ' \?'
  let tail = ' \?' . s:rx(close) . '\s*$'
  let lines = getline(a:first, a:last)
  let code = filter(copy(lines), 'v:val =~# "\\S"')
  if empty(code)
    return
  endif
  " Uncomment only when every non-blank line is already commented.
  " Otherwise a half-commented block would flip into a mess.
  let strip = 1
  let indent = 999
  for line in code
    let strip = strip && line =~# head
    let indent = min([indent, match(line, '\S')])
  endfor
  let n = a:first
  for line in lines
    if line =~# '\S'
      if strip
        let out = substitute(line, head, '\1', '')
        let out = close ==# '' ? out : substitute(out, tail, '', '')
      else
        let body = open . ' ' . strpart(line, indent)
        let out = strpart(line, 0, indent) . (close ==# '' ? body : body . ' ' . close)
      endif
      call setline(n, out)
    endif
    let n += 1
  endfor
endfunction

function! s:opfunc(type) abort
  call s:toggle(line("'["), line("']"))
endfunction

nnoremap <silent> gcc :<C-u>call <SID>toggle(line('.'), line('.'))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> gc :<C-u>set operatorfunc=<SID>opfunc<CR>g@
xnoremap <silent> gc :<C-u>call <SID>toggle(line("'<"), line("'>"))<CR>

@start schema.sql
CREATE TABLE refunds (
  id CHAR(36) NOT NULL,
  order_id CHAR(36) NOT NULL,
  amount_minor INT NOT NULL,
  settled_at DATETIME NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (id)
);

CREATE INDEX refunds_order_id_idx ON refunds (order_id);

DROP TABLE refunds_old;

@expected schema.sql
CREATE TABLE refunds (
  id CHAR(36) NOT NULL,
  order_id CHAR(36) NOT NULL,
  amount_minor INT NOT NULL,
  settled_at DATETIME NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (id)
);

CREATE INDEX refunds_order_id_idx ON refunds (order_id);

-- DROP TABLE refunds_old;

@start README.md
# Payments service

Run `npm run db:migrate` before starting the service. The seed data is
for local development only.

The legacy /v1/refunds endpoint is still available for the mobile client.

Webhook signatures are checked against the secret in the environment.

@expected README.md
# Payments service

Run `npm run db:migrate` before starting the service. The seed data is
for local development only.

<!-- The legacy /v1/refunds endpoint is still available for the mobile client. -->

Webhook signatures are checked against the secret in the environment.

